The graph has only 6 edges, compared with a maximum of 15.

The execution results show:

Both representations produce the same BFS order: A → B → C → D → E → F.

Both produce the same DFS order under the chosen alphabetical neighbour ordering: A → B → D → E → F → C.

Searching the vertex labels sequentially requires the same number of comparisons.

However, edge checking is O(1) with a matrix, compared with O(degree(v)) using a list.

The adjacency matrix uses 36 matrix cells, whereas the adjacency list needs only about 18 vertex/edge entries for this example.

For larger sparse graphs, the difference becomes much more significant because the matrix grows as 
V
2
, while the list grows as 
V
+
E
.

Conclusion
For a sparse social network, the adjacency list is more suitable.

Its main advantage is its O(V + E) space requirement and efficient BFS/DFS traversal of O(V + E). It stores only the connections that actually exist rather than allocating space for every possible pair of vertices.

The adjacency matrix is preferable when very frequent edge-existence checks are required, because an edge can be checked in O(1) time. However, for the sparse network described here, the adjacency list provides better storage efficiency and traversal behaviour.
